<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moravia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moravia is a historical region in the eastern part of the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. It was a crown land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and part of the Austrian half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> it formed one of the empire's most industrialised areas, with textile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and engineering industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moravia.jpg" alt="Moravia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tadeáš Bednarz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overview_of_Mikulov_from_Svat%C3%BD_Kope%C4%8Dek_2020_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gregor Mendel, an Augustinian friar, presented his pea-plant experiments on heredity in Brünn, <b>Moravia</b>, in 1865; they were largely ignored until they were rediscovered in 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the industrialised provinces of Bohemia and <b>Moravia</b>, the Škoda works at Plzeň, textile mills, sugar refineries and glassworks made the region one of the most productive industrial zones in continental Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In central Europe its fall opened the way for the Frankish and Bavarian expansion into Pannonia and for the Christian missions of Salzburg and Aquileia among the Slavs, which in turn prepared the ground for Great <b>Moravia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…missions turned potential enemies into clients, neutralised threats and carried Byzantine culture far beyond the reach of its armies, as shown by the mission of Saints Cyril and Methodius to the Slavs of <b>Moravia</b> in 863 and the conversion of Bulgaria (864) and of Kievan Rus' (c. 988).</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of Bohemia and <b>Moravia</b>, defended Slovakia against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with Poland over Teschen, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When disciples of Cyril and Methodius, among them Clement and Naum, were expelled from <b>Moravia</b> in 885–886, Boris I received them and gave them the means to teach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
